/*******************************************************************************
	Kant in kant: the plan
*******************************************************************************/
Goal: jitted turtles all the way down. rule() fires its parse; the parse fires
its action; the action BUILDS an instruct and returns it as its label. Running
the label runs the program. Proof that kant is a language that writes itself.

/*******************************************************************************
	Two phases, never mixed
*******************************************************************************/
Build time: a rule action fires during the parse and builds an instruct. It
evaluates nothing (bear-trap #34: building must never fire).
The one exception: code that DECIDES the parse (accept or reject a match) runs
at parse time, because the parse cannot wait for it.
Until kant can write a rejection (open question 1), a rule's parse decision
may live on its own small rule beneath it, in C++ -- the two-step. The worked
example, as it stood until SEQ 469:
    SHORTCUTs=[-+~`$_:,]+ ruleMethod=shortcutTest;   decides (rejects ++ -- $$)
    SHORTCUt  shortcut=SHORTCUTs;                    builds (kant action)
SEQ 469 dropped the decision on the new road instead of moving it: `,` already
separates a shortcut from an operator in a print line (Tony), so ++ is two
shortcuts there. SHORTCUt is one rule again, with no C++:
    SHORTCUt  shortcut+=[-+~`$_:,];                  builds (kant action)
The + on the label reads as [set]+ (one label per run, traced) and is inert:
no row tells it from shortcut=[...], since shortcuts printed one by one print
what the run prints. The old road keeps its refusal (shortcutTest stays for
aCTionShortcuT). The two-step pattern stands for the next real decider.
Run time: the instruct runs. testOP is every instruct's method: if a field has
a method, run it; if not, hand it back as is. A plain value is a valid chain end.

/*******************************************************************************
	The shapes
*******************************************************************************/
Expression chain   op target argument, a tree (EXPRESSIOn builds these today).
seq instruct       op = seq, argument = a LIST of statement instructs.
                   opSeq walks the list in order. An action body is a seq.
                   Built.
print instruct     op = printList, argument = the list of items. A shortcut
                   item is its own instruct, op = printShortcut. Built.
if instruct        op = ifThen, argument = [condition, then, else?]. opIf runs
                   the condition and one arm, or none. Built (SEQ 473).
loop instructs     whileLoop [condition, statement], doLoop [statement,
                   condition], forLoop [cursor, source, statement, <-?]; each op
                   catches break and continue. Built (SEQ 478 / 480 / 482).
iterate instruct   op = iterateOn, argument = [cursor, source, attributes 0/1,
                   members 0/1]; opIterate calls iterateSetup, the setup both
                   roads share. WHILe and ++ / -- do the walking. Built (486).
branch instruct    op = branchSignal, argument = the keyword. opBranch sets
                   the signal (break 1, continue 2). return refuses by name
                   until action bodies exist (rung 5). Built (SEQ 476).
block instruct     a { } builds a seq, as STARt does. Built (SEQ 476).
Every value-producing instruct hands back its result on its own node, never a
shared slot. Today's exceptions, each on purpose:
    opPrintList ends in opPrint and hands back trueResult (the shared sentinel);
    opSeq hands back its argument list;
    opPrintShortcut hands back null, so the print loop skips it as an item;
    opIf hands back the arm's result, or nothing when no arm ran (R3, the
    yield ruling); the loop ops, opBranch and opIterate hand back nothing.

/*******************************************************************************
	Branches: signals, not targets
*******************************************************************************/
break, continue, return raise a signal (branchKind). Nobody computes a target.
The nearest loop instruct reads break and continue after each statement;
the action's own seq reads return.
DESIGN RULE, "the landing finds itself" (Tony, 2026-10-30): each op declares
which signals it catches -- break and continue are caught by the nearest loop,
return by the nearest action boundary. A branch knows nothing of where it lands.
IF and seq pass signals through: a seq stops its list when a signal is set and
leaves it set (it clears before each statement, as the old road's block does);
an if runs one arm and hands the signal on. A stray break with no loop ends its
seq and the next statement runs (SEQ 477 R0).

