/*******************************************************************************
	The new grammar
*******************************************************************************/
A new incantation beside grammar (kantGrammar). How it is reached today: a file
registers it in fILEs and includes it (printFormatT's prelude is the pattern),
and a new rule runs by being called by name, rule("text") (R6). Swapping
Grokking out of the search list changes name lookups, not the driver (seal 234),
so that swap is dropped for now.
Setup, the bootstrap and anything already compiled stay on Grokking. No change
to existing code or interpreter.
Every new rule action is kant and returns an instruct, except a parse decision
(see Two phases).
Rules for every new rule:
    - No meaning carried in C++ flags. If an item means something, that meaning
      is an instruct, an op or an attribute, never a bit the engine remembers.
    - Resolve operands at run time, never at build.
    - A built instruct holds copies (copyOf), never the action's own locals: a
      local is per-call scratch, so an instruct that kept one would read the
      last call's value (bear-trap 50; PRINt's itemsMint, SHORTCUt's scText).
    - A parse decision cannot share a rule with the build: ruleMethod= on a
      rule displaces its code={} (measured, SEQ 462). The decider goes on its
      own small rule beneath (the two-step; SHORTCUTs was the example until
      SEQ 469 dropped its decision).
    - A list is known by its TAG, never by isLIST / isLisT. Adjacent operands
      (qa qb) are one operand, a list tagged ADJACENt that EXPRESSIOn's action
      hands up; PRINt adds its members as separate items (SEQ 467 / 468).

